  "name": "Question 10",
  "problem": "Given that $Q(5,-1,9)$ lies on the plane $\u03a0:r\\cdot \u27e83,-6,-2\u27e9=3$, find the shortest distance from $P(7,-2,8)$ to $\u03a0.$",
  "worked_solution": "First, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =\u27e85,-1,9\u27e9-\u27e87,-2,8\u27e9 \\\\  & =\u27e8-2,1,1\u27e9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the equation of a plane, we get a normal vector $n=\u27e83,-6,-2\u27e9.$\n\nFinally, the shortest distance from the point $P$ to the plane $\u03a0$ is the absolute value of the scalar projection of $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ onto the normal vector of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =|{\\text{comp}}_{n}\\,\\overset{\\to }{PQ}| \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\cdot n|}{|n|} \\\\  & =\\frac{|(-2)\\cdot 3+1\\cdot (-6)+1\\cdot (-2)|}{\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+(-6)^{2}+(-2)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|-6-6-2|}{\\sqrt{9+36+4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|-14|}{\\sqrt{49}} \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{7} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
